The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
err_free_skb:
	dev_dstats_tx_dropped(priv->ndev);
	dev_kfree_skb_any(skb);
```
Reason:
When a DMA mapping error occurs in `emac_tx_mem_map()`, the error handling path jumps to `err_free_skb`. This path only frees the socket buffer (`skb`) but does not free the DMA mappings that were already created for earlier fragments. Each successful call to `emac_tx_map_frag()` maps a DMA buffer and, if a later fragment fails, those mappings remain unreleased, causing a DMA mapping leak.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
	i = old_head;
	while (i != head) {
		emac_free_tx_buf(priv, i);

		if (++i == tx_ring->total_cnt)
			i = 0;
	}
```
Reason:
The added loop iterates over all descriptors that were successfully mapped (from `old_head` to `head`, wrapping around the ring buffer). For each descriptor, it calls `emac_free_tx_buf()` to unmap the DMA buffers and free any associated socket buffers (except that the overall `skb` is freed later). This ensures that all previously created DMA mappings are properly released before freeing the `skb`, eliminating the leak.

